The Dirty Pipe Linux kernel vulnerability is a bug in the Linux kernel that allows an attacker to write arbitrary data to a file. This bug affects Linux kernels up to and including version 5.8.
The bug is caused by a problem in the way the kernel handles pipes. A pipe is a special type of file that is used to connect two processes. When a process writes to a pipe, the data is stored in a buffer. The other process can then read the data from the buffer.
The bug occurs because the kernel does not properly validate the buffer before writing data to it. An attacker can exploit this bug by creating a pipe and then writing arbitrary data to the pipe. The kernel will then write the data to the file that the pipe is connected to.
